I'm sure that someone out there will port RedHat 7.x and on to the 
Alpha.  It's not that big of a deal, really.  Now, if you want actual 
SUPPORT, that's a big deal.  Of course, since you have the source code, 
you can fix any bugs you find (or pay someone else to do it).

> I could still go from 7 to 7.1, but RedHat doesn't advertise any
> products for Alpha at all. I have an inquiry in to sales, but my guess
> is that they are dropping all support as well, and don't want new
> customers.
> 
> What are others doing?

Well, first of all, RedHat isn't the end all and be all of Linux.  You have

any number of choices: SuSE, Debian, Mandrake, and even Slackware have 
been ported to the Alpha.  Not only that, but you can switch to FreeBSD, 
NetBSD, or OpenBSD.  If all those sound boring to you, then there's 
always Tru64, OpenVMS, or NT4.

If someone donated a DS20 to me, I'd sit around all day porting RedHat 
8.x to the Alpha and fixing every bug I found.  But since nobody is 
likely to donate something that expensive, I'll just keep tinkering 
around with my Multia and upgrade important applications the 
old-fashioned way -- downloading the source code and compiling it myself!

There's no reason you need RedHat to precompile Apache for you.  Just go 
to www.apache.org and get the latest source code.

Back in the early days of Linux, we didn't even have distributions.  We 
had to do everything ourselves.  So, don't panic just because some 
distribution has dropped support for the Alpha.  You can make your own 
distribution.  It's not as hard as it sounds, since 99% of the work has 
already been done for you (porting a kernel and compiler).



